WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.389 --> 00:00:03.701
We're back with Kym Whitley and Finesse Mitchell!

2
00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:05.371
(crowd cheers)
(applause)

3
00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:08.050
I am seriously having the best time.

4
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.670
Finesse, it's your story.

5
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:10.650
It's my story.

6
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:11.660
Okay, listen up.

7
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.430
Now, to all my parents out there.

8
00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.400
Now, it's been a little rough for a while now,

9
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.510
but you're doing a great job, and you know it,

10
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:19.919
give yourselves a pat on the back.

11
00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.250
(applause)

12
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.380
No matter how you feel inside, you're doing a good job.

13
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.150
This pandemic isn't easy.

14
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.840
But a new study finds that many parents actually do

15
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.630
kind of feel like they are superheroes when it comes to

16
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.760
their kids.

17
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.640
The Good News Network reports, a poll of 2000 parents found

18
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.430
the majority said that they discovered superhuman reflexes

19
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.290
within two months of having their kids.

20
00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.123
That's me.

21
00:00:43.123 --> 00:00:46.310 line:15%
Like the crazy baby catching reflex.

22
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:47.143 line:15%
I've done that.

23
00:00:47.143 --> 00:00:49.930 line:15%
Or having the ability to have eyes in the back of your head.

24
00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:50.820 line:15%
We've all done that.

25
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.890 line:15%
You know when your child is sick just by looking at them,

26
00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.640 line:15%
we all have that power.

27
00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.170 line:15%
Kym, Drew, what superhuman powers do you think you guys have

28
00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.470 line:15%
attained since having a baby?

29
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.803 line:15%
It's like sleep deprivation with a cape.

30
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.013 line:15%
Let me tell you something.

31
00:01:06.013 --> 00:01:10.130 line:15%
The nights I stay up and still go to work the next day,

32
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.130 line:15%
it was unbelievable.

33
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.320 line:15%
Your eyes are burning, but you power through.

34
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.990 line:15%
I have super hearing.

35
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.730 line:15%
I heard the baby, I'm telling you,

36
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.510 line:15%
way down the hallway, I just heard (coos lightly)

37
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.600 line:15%
I was like, "Oh my God, he needs me!"

38
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.480
There's so many things you didn't know as a parent.

39
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.817
I adopted my son at four days old and I was like,

40
00:01:31.817 --> 00:01:33.220
"Oh my God, I didn't have him.

41
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.760
I'm not gonna have all the things that mothers have"

42
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.758
I got every last bit of it, yes.

43
00:01:39.758 --> 00:01:42.008
(applause)

44
00:01:43.828 --> 00:01:44.716
Wanna know what my superpower is?

45
00:01:44.716 --> 00:01:46.956
What is it, Finesse, 'cause you're a great dad?

46
00:01:46.956 --> 00:01:49.240
I have a three year old, I have a seven year old,

47
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.290
both girls.

48
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.836
My superpower is restraint.

49
00:01:52.836 --> 00:01:53.680
(Ross and Kym laugh)

50
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.540
That is my superpower.
How so?

51
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.930
I was thinking about it.

52
00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.050
Just so that when they argue with each other or they try to

53
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.490
pull those stunts because they don't want to go to bed.

54
00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.490
That whole, "Elle, Eva, let's, you know, it's time.

55
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:12.240
We're not gonna play these games" You know what I mean?

56
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.820
But see, I wasn't raised that way because my mom used to be

57
00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.790
like, "Get in the bed, I'ma count, 10, nine, eight"

58
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.590
You know as a kid, you wait 'til they get to about three,

59
00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.360
and then you take off running because you know

60
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.920
what comes at one.

61
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.770
But if I start counting with these new age kids,

62
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.384
they finish counting for me.

63
00:02:30.384 --> 00:02:31.570
(laughter)

64
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:34.240
They start clapping like, "We did it!

65
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.073
We got all the way to one"

66
00:02:35.073 --> 00:02:38.380
And I'm like, "You're not playing the game right"

67
00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.820
But they don't even understand or get it.

68
00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.610
Patience, so your super power is patience.

69
00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.660
What a good dad you are.

70
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.070
And girl dad, that's so fun.

71
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.300
Two girl club.

72
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.789
Yes, two girls, two girls.

73
00:02:50.789 --> 00:02:52.370
I love it.

74
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.640
Am I gonna have kids?

75
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.060
I don't know if I'm gonna have kids.

76
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.740
We had our, Wellington's nephews over

77
00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.781
and I got, they're like 10 or 30, I don't know.

78
00:03:00.781 --> 00:03:03.780
(Kym and Finesse laugh)

79
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.613
I don't know how old they are.

80
00:03:04.613 --> 00:03:06.567
They're somewhere between 10 and 25.

81
00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:08.960
And I was like, "Hello, how are you?"

82
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.130
I don't know how to be around kids necessarily.

83
00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.450
I don't know, I don't have, but I think that's a super power

84
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.560
you get when you have kids, suddenly you just.

85
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.310
Like my mom, her superpower.

86
00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.020
She had like a thermometer in her hand.

87
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.130
I'd always be like, "do I have a fever?"

88
00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.850
And she'd just touch you and be like, "No.

89
00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.720
Yes, no" There's those things as a parent, you get.

90
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.460
I do not have those things right now.

91
00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:27.580
Could happen, Rossy.

92
00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.413
It could.

93
00:03:28.413 --> 00:03:29.850
Well, if it happens, it'll be on purpose.

94
00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.968
We're not gonna have any mistakes, that's for sure.

95
00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:33.440
(everyone laughs)

96
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.870
Will be no surprise babies here.

97
00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:36.703
Gross.

98
00:03:36.703 --> 00:03:38.690
All right.
(laughing continues)

99
00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.903
All right, well, you know, let me move on.

100
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.210
I'm moving on.

101
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.740
All right, you know, life is full of milestones,

102
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.300
weddings, having babies, baby showers,

103
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.900
and now there's another celebration people are having,

104
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.360
a divorce party.

105
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.340
Yeah, okay.

106
00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.600
The blog PureWow reports, people are throwing

107
00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.590
their newly divorced pals a little soiree to celebrate

108
00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:00.510
a fresh start.

109
00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.740
So if you've got a friend who could use a pump up party,

110
00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.780
here are some tips to throw a divorce party for yourself.

111
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.480 line:15%
Keep it intimate, they say, keep it upbeat.

112
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.740 line:15%
They say, get it catered, nobody wants to cook.

113
00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.120 line:15%
Buy a big cake and bring some thoughtful gifts.

114
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.418 line:15%
I say, get a pinata made that looks like your ex.

115
00:04:17.418 --> 00:04:19.248 line:15%
(all laugh)

116
00:04:19.248 --> 00:04:20.683 line:15%
Now that's a party.

117
00:04:23.189 --> 00:04:24.480 line:15%
That is a party.

118
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.150
Here's what I was just thinking.

119
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.500
Sometimes that can get a little messy only because

120
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.500
the people, your friends and your ex-wife's friends,

121
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.110
they're the same friends a lot of times.

122
00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.690
So you're inviting half of those friends

123
00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:38.940
to your divorce party.

124
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.520
Which means they're telling the other friends,

125
00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.020
which means it gets back to the person

126
00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:42.853
that's being divorced.

127
00:04:42.853 --> 00:04:45.010
And she told two friends, and they told two friends.

128
00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:48.900
And in divorce, both sides aren't usually as happy,

129
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.450
equally happy, somebody either still wants to get

130
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.140
back together or somebody's, you know,

131
00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.150
hanging on to hope, so that I don't know,

132
00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.440
somebody that's just like, "I'm getting divorced, finally!"

133
00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.910
That just makes things worse,

134
00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.490
especially when kids are involved.

135
00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:02.740
I think you should invite your friends

136
00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:03.950
from college, you know what I mean?

137
00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:05.360
The friends you've had for a million years.

138
00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:06.880
They gotta be the original friends that told you

139
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.180
not to get married in the first place.

140
00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.753
Yeah.
(Ross laughs)

141
00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.090
Friends you had to let go because you got married.

142
00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.629
That's it, Finesse.

143
00:05:16.629 --> 00:05:19.212 line:15%
(upbeat music)

